   3.936267161247027*^9, 3.936267162197678*^9}, {3.936267285195869*^9, 
   3.936267285420269*^9}, {3.936267543396661*^9, 3.936267546483736*^9}, 
   3.936268454859458*^9, {3.9362685528289833`*^9, 3.936268555450248*^9}, {
   3.936271509135705*^9, 3.9362715106876183`*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[74]:=",ExpressionUUID->"a2a4df83-7b31-4f7b-b0f3-771b9e0647ce"],

Cell["\<\
We can also reduce the network to its 12 first reactions. This is enough for \
the first light elements. 
It consists in the first 12 reactions of the tabulated reactions (those in \
the file TabulatedReactionsFile).
Furthermore, if $ReducedNetwork is selected, no extra analytic reactions are \
added so that we really have only a total of 12+1 reactions (because we also \
have weak interactions).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.937272216448125*^9},ExpressionUUID->"88be4a4e-d418-4ae1-849c-\
3293e56f8f86"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$ReducedNetwork", "=", "False"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.936069345643951*^9, 3.9360693461373*^9}, {
  3.936069991879552*^9, 3.936069994038876*^9}, {3.936070285397822*^9, 
  3.936070285966302*^9}, {3.936087116483869*^9, 3.936087117151601*^9}, {
  3.9360877164021273`*^9, 3.9360877167887163`*^9}, {3.936088917065736*^9, 
  3.936088919693541*^9}, {3.93626782833031*^9, 3.936267828895653*^9}, {
  3.9362684523518353`*^9, 3.936268452886188*^9}, {3.936270625237322*^9, 
  3.936270625729146*^9}, {3.9362715057126207`*^9, 3.9362715065269423`*^9}, {
  3.936289048547275*^9, 3.9362890489692087`*^9}, {3.936293678471551*^9, 
  3.936293679113415*^9}, {3.9367826794063787`*^9, 3.936782680734264*^9}, {
  3.93678852824335*^9, 3.936788528726893*^9}, {3.988932466631729*^9, 
  3.988932467027802*^9}, {3.9889329397129517`*^9, 3.988932940419845*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[75]:=",ExpressionUUID->"668fc8ed-4c51-4166-908a-9b77f3627658"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"SmallNuclearNetworkSize", "=", "12"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9361648768942966`*^9, 3.936164880646248*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[76]:=",ExpressionUUID->"6d7b6c57-0e4f-4288-8163-d8692340f6bf"],

Cell["Monte-Carlo uncertainty estimation options", "Text",
 CellDingbat->"\[FilledSquare]",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.715920372139921*^9, 3.715920409490917*^9}, {
  3.715920440783332*^9, 3.715920444857109*^9}, {3.7226089346025248`*^9, 
  3.7226089490163107`*^9}},
 FontSize->16,
 FontVariations->{
 "Underline"->True},ExpressionUUID->"433350d1-4509-4811-99a4-824888ecdc33"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$RandomNuclearRates", "=", "False"}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$MaxVariationRate", "=", "1000"}], ";"}]}], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.700386367200856*^9, 3.700386367369774*^9}, 
   3.719928709272653*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[77]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c9af897a-6950-4c04-a340-60240444ff6e"],

Cell["\<\
If $RandomNuclearRates is True, then each time we build the equations of the \
nuclear network, we generate rates with a log normal distribution according \
to the \[OpenCurlyQuote]f\[CloseCurlyQuote] specified (see Eq. 4.5 of [Coc et \
al. 2014] for definition of f). 
We limit f to the values 1/$MaxVariationRate<f<$MaxVariationRate .\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"34270de5-5d0b-4541-9d65-193eed29ab62"],

Cell["Rescaling of some rates", "Text",
 CellDingbat->"\[FilledSquare]",
 FontSize->18,
 FontVariations->{
 "Underline"->True},ExpressionUUID->"5c8f2821-589c-434d-8770-ae61148fcfdd"],

Cell["\<\
It is possible to rescale some rates by choosing their rescaling factor \
below. First, the option $RescaleSomeRates must be set to True otherwise the \
rescaling factors are ignored.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c75a20b2-9425-4874-b471-48ed88507806"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$RescaleSomeRates", "=", "False"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellLabel->"In[79]:=",ExpressionUUID->"61ecf06d-f3b7-41cc-ac86-5654650b1638"],

Cell["\<\
For each reaction whose rate we want to rescale, we need to know the exact \
name of the reaction. The names of reactions are collected in tables below in \
the core of the notebook. For a reaction of the type a + b -> c + d, the name \
is \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]abTOcd\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. However one needs \
to know the order of the initial particles and of the final particles, and to \
put correctly the photons if they appear. Hence it is recommended not to \
guess the name of the rate, but to properly read it from the Tables below \
located in the section \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Collecting all reaction rates\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote].
For instance the reaction He3 + t > He4 + d  (we use d for H2 and t for H3 \
and a for He4) has name He3tTOHe4d. The factor to modify it is therefore \
He3tTOHe4dFactor.

As an example, we show how to introduce a rescaling factor for the d + p -> \
He3 + \[Gamma],  d + d -> He3 + n, and d + d -> H3 + p  reactions hereafter. 
If we want to vary other rates, one needs only to set the numerical value of \
their associated factors .\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0e0504fd-3dfe-441d-80c4-b86a41425abd"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"If", "[", 
   RowBox[{"$RescaleSomeRates", ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dpTOHe3gFactor", "=", "1"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"ddTOHe3nFactor", "=", "1"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"ddTOtpFactor", "=", "1"}], ";"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellLabel->"In[80]:=",ExpressionUUID->"087b005f-a214-4197-9c4c-f84f084f14fc"],

Cell["\<\
Note that this procedure is only valid for variation of rates which are given \
as tables in the external file. If the user wants to vary a rate whose form \
is analytic, it is sufficient to add a factor \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]ReactionFactor\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in the \
definition of the analytic forward rate (forward[T9_]:= ....). It is then \
advised to write here (so that all modifications can be turned on and off \
without forgetting that some rates have been modified)

If[$RescaleSomeRates,
ReactionFactor=100;,
ReactionFactor=1;
]

We recall that the syntax for If/then/else is

If[Condition, 
commandtrue;,
commandfalse;
]\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5d518a9b-e42d-4e58-9bd0-3170b0b424cb"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["QED corrections (pair production) to nuclear rates",
 FontSize->16,
 FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}]], "Text",
 CellDingbat->
  "\[FilledSquare]",ExpressionUUID->"980c1b3c-7aeb-435c-843f-7657a9adf011"],

Cell["\<\
If this options is set to True, then we rescale some rates due to pair \
productions in the final state. 
See [Pitrou&Pospelov 2020].\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d3fdf3c8-d514-4165-bbe6-4259088c094a"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$NuclearRatesQEDCorrections", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", " ", 
   RowBox[{"recommended", " ", "value", " ", "is", " ", "True"}], " ", 
   "*)"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.861260253121438*^9, 3.861260254666264*^9}, {
  3.861260469458742*^9, 3.861260476730362*^9}, {3.861260697494587*^9, 
  3.861260698077643*^9}, {3.861261699162174*^9, 3.861261699648961*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[81]:=",ExpressionUUID->"7d65655c-57e4-4fc4-bbfe-6b6436293106"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Weak rates options", "Subsection",
 InitializationCell->
  True,ExpressionUUID->"90f9ae96-7414-42b5-85be-2a318b460a79"],

Cell["\<\
Since the weak interaction rates, that is the rates of weak reactions of the \
type n + \[Nu] <-> p + e and associated reactions, depend only on temperature, 
these can be computed once and for all so as to explore the dependence in \
cosmological parameters or other parameters. 
  
If $RecomputeWeakRates is set to True, the code recomputes the weak rates. 
Otherwise it reads them from files previously stored. If the file does not \
exist, it recomputes the rates.

If $ParallelWeakRates=True this is done using parallelization over the \
various CPUs that Mathematica can detect (be aware that when $NEVO=TRue , it \
slows things done because of badly sharing memory. I cannot find the reason.)\
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.935543627331623*^9, 3.935543643983611*^9}, {
  3.936404628014121*^9, 
  3.936404646494323*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6b12bf47-f342-4594-a77b-\
70f24963cab3"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$RecomputeWeakRates", "=", "False"}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$ParallelWeakRates", "=", "False"}], ";"}]}], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJwdxU0og2EcAPDXDhKxNZpilq8iK8uK5LPnsDLl4J2l5GMt22IxsXDwUdts
B9Guu9EcloP2nYwptgMythO2HDaT2Bs5SNOW5/kffv3qlDpaxaIoqgYjv2ok
G1WeDHq/lcGJjoCgGu8W3MHJPeksH++3FGvJ16v0ocGXQSO7GpjnHi/3nmZQ
TD0DWw0fQfJYdw4+0a7OrYQyiM5uwdbppkUyxe6HF9ft+yH8b6UH5vJunGF8
Q22Fi2xiD06mChg0/TIB/9S4VGR9Yx5WRIq8Y8MMeotIYOVRNE6O21PwQt/h
owL/pAjA8oaBZj3NIGp+HS6xCdRk72g73NmbazPifTRHTC6byl6SzdtlIXKy
q55jwi+7RXDr98+ZBS8QFgbJYVbPtQPPLx2CxVSLVipjkNPfA18EHh7u8eZc
GlYmroQx/IE3Af9xdaIU3ra0CWt3zlmf+LwjChe57TslcgYF0x547fjZyMPr
41/wP9N47+Q=
  "],
 CellLabel->"In[82]:=",ExpressionUUID->"32192ced-29d4-4cb7-94d4-d4b4d5c80535"],

Cell["\<\
There are several booleans corresponding to the different types of \
corrections which can be considered in these weak rates. 
The name of the file used for storing the rates is built out of these \
booleans. 

Since the corrections do not add linearly, there are in principle many \
choices of corrections, but the only meaningful
choices are those without any corrections and those with all corrections \
included. 
Or maybe those with just one correction added, so as to check its amplitude.\
\>", "Text",
 InitializationCell->
  True,ExpressionUUID->"128f1039-5a8b-4833-a915-c9fa30b5d8a0"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$RadiativeCorrections", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}], 
  "\n"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$ResummedLogsRadiativeCorrections", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$RelativisticFermiFunction", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}]}], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellLabel->"In[84]:=",ExpressionUUID->"a611470b-64ec-481a-91c1-0f369bdca23a"],

Cell["\<\
1) If $RadiativeCorrections is set to True, we use Coulomb and radiative \
corrections (see section III.E of companion paper. The corrections are \
implemented in Eqs. 101 and 104.).
If $ResummedLogsRadiativeCorrections is set to True we use Eq. 15 of \
[Czarnecki et al 2004] which amounts to resumming some higher order radiative \
corrections, and this is also Eq. B35 of the companion paper.

If $ResummedLogsRadiativeCorrections is False we use simply Eq. 7 of the same \
reference, which corresponds to Eq. 103 and B30 of the companion paper.
If $RelativisticFermiFunction is True we use the relativistic Fermi function \
(Eq. 5 in [Ivanov 2012]), which corresponds to Eq. 100 of the companion \
paper. Otherwise we use the standard non-relativistic Fermi function, given \
by Eq. 99 of the companion paper.\
\>", "Text",
 InitializationCell->
  True,ExpressionUUID->"03a9bddb-fbde-42a0-8cac-fa6554e242e4"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$RadiativeThermal", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
    "Recommended", " ", "True", " ", "but", " ", "it", " ", "is", " ", "much",
      " ", 
     RowBox[{"slower", ".", " ", "Since"}], " ", "the", " ", "modification", 
     " ", "brought", " ", "by", " ", "these", " ", "corrections", " ", "is", 
     " ", "very", " ", "small"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"setting", " ", "False", " ", "is", " ", 
     RowBox[{"acceptable", "."}]}]}], "*)"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$CorrectionBremsstrahlung", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}]}], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGAQBmIQPWWG2iKpzW8c1T5bg+kDm6ZmywDprPTFYHqbZUQB
iC7bkV8EomWm8Vwu2vrG8SS/Apg+ylDPWwykM+u6wbRBaroDiBYorwLTQVvm
79my541jVOpaMC21ynHRkSNvHOemTQXTiTMbNh4F0hZV88B03pmqMwmBbx3X
WN4D0378DLolQW8dY/Tb9UF0VbvOtnogXdNoDaavnAw0bgbSWYezwbT46o4j
ILr50FQw3bN6pUALkG7bsQtMz+Nr3tcOpI+dngymF3lcPbUCSGs4vgDTKSWS
OZ7Bbx11pFXBtKnzj0zPHW8dTzcdzgbRAD8JnDA=
  "],
 CellLabel->"In[87]:=",ExpressionUUID->"6ac069ea-a407-4007-8558-782c21e48c84"],

Cell["\<\
2) If $RadiativeThermal set to True, the thermal radiative corrections are \
taken into account. 
The first expressions date back from [Dicus et al.]. However other \
expressions were derived subsequently in [Lopez&Turner 1998], [Cambier et \
al.] and [Esposito et al. 1999] among other references. [Kernan] pointed \
typos and compared the differing results of [Cambier et al.] and [Dicus et \
al.]. These were correctly computed in [Brown&Sawyer].
In the companion paper, the thermal radiative corrections are given by Eq. \
108 with the various contributions given by Eqs. 109-113. However, it is \
easier to compute the first contribution of 108 using Eqs. 109 (with \
definition B41), but to compute the second and third contributions of 108 \
using Eqs. B50-B51.

We showed in companion paper that Bremsstrahlung corrections need also to be \
added to be fully consistent with [Brown&Sawyer]. This is controlled by the \
boolean $CorrectionBremsstrahlung. If $RadiativeThermal is set to True, then \
these bremsstrahlung corrections (corresponding to Eqs. 107a and 107b in \
companion paper with the definitions B48-B49) are also incorporated if \
$CorrectionBremsstrahlung is True.\
\>", "Text",
 InitializationCell->
  True,ExpressionUUID->"0da09c0c-15ea-4724-b53a-98d1206dd268"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$FiniteNucleonMass", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellLabel->"In[89]:=",ExpressionUUID->"e3fefdd7-c84a-46cd-98a8-9a7d2060bdb4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "3) If $FiniteNucleonMass is set to True, we take into account the finite \
mass of nucleons by keeping corrections in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", "/", 
    SubscriptBox["M", "n"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "57db28de-58bd-47a0-99a5-40d72fc5fb34"],
 " in the collision integrals of the weak rates.\nOur method is described in \
the companion paper and differs from earlier literature.\nThere is a \
suboption for these finite mass corrections  which is"
}], "Text",
 InitializationCell->
  True,ExpressionUUID->"83799182-eee5-49e7-8d15-cd12a8722ebc"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$CoupledFMandRC", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellLabel->"In[90]:=",ExpressionUUID->"aa8f36d1-5fb2-4796-bef6-906d51f26514"],

Cell["\<\
If $CoupledFMandRC is False, finite mass corrections are computed from the \
Born results with no radiative null temperature corrections. If this is set \
to True, the finite mass corrections are applied to the rates on which the \
Coulomb and radiative corrections are taken into account. True is the advised \
value. 

The expressions implemented are Eqs. 114 when $CoupledFMandRC is False, with \
the definition B23. If $CoupledFMandRC is True, then we use Eqs. 115 with the \
Fermi and radiative corrections corresponding to the above choices for \
radiative corrections.\
\>", "Text",
 InitializationCell->
  True,ExpressionUUID->"9acd1ffc-2942-46f6-b373-b0bab5aee489"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "4) Mass shifts due to QED plasma effects are ",
 StyleBox["ALREADY",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " taken into account in thermal radiative corrections.\nHowever it is \
possible to turn the option $QEDMassShift to true to check how this affects \
the rate when taken individually.\nApart to satisfy this curiosity, ",
 StyleBox["this option should be set to False",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " in all cases."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.718622268666133*^9, 3.7186222860350647`*^9}, {
  3.718622957048277*^9, 3.7186230151103487`*^9}, {3.722609352433152*^9, 
  3.722609370793644*^9}, {3.724925157208601*^9, 
  3.724925159426754*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"69b8a6aa-b70a-4e43-90d8-\
f9e51b3e608a"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$QEDMassShift", "=", "False"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.718622288534856*^9, 3.718622303892522*^9}, {
  3.718622585396584*^9, 3.718622586005734*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[91]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c885a24a-6786-4b0c-a29a-9444deeac869"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Plasma physics options", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"8d73ab9e-ac02-4130-93f5-f82e653946fd"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$QEDPlasmaCorrections", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$CompleteQEDPressure", "=", "True"}], ";"}], 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"$QEDOe3", " ", "=", " ", "True"}], ";"}], " ", 
  RowBox[{"(*", 
   RowBox[{"Recommended", " ", "True"}], "*)"}]}]}], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.861259612382286*^9, 3.861259612941025*^9}, {
  3.861260482427253*^9, 3.861260482907577*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[92]:=",ExpressionUUID->"9a07fb33-889d-4da2-a43e-778e2afe93fe"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If $QEDPlasmaCorrections is set to True, the QED corrections to the \
pressure and the energy density are taken into account. This affects for \
instance the expansion rate via the Friedmann equation. \nExpressions can be \
found in [Lopez&Turner 1998], [Mangano et al. 2001] or in [Heckler 1994]. See \
also the companion paper where Eqs. 48-52 are used inside Eq. 55 to modify \
the entropy and Eq. 58 to modify the energy density.\nIf $CompleteQEDPressure \
is False, the subdominant term is ignored. Otherwise it is included. We \
checked it is so subdominant that it does not change the results.\nIf $QEDOe3 \
is True, then order ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["e", "3"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "84334a77-0cd3-4752-8878-bb30a03fcbab"],
 " corrections to the Plasma (to P and \[Rho]) are included, Following [",
 StyleBox["Bennett et. al. 2019].",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.809769617071743*^9},ExpressionUUID->"59f3461f-ea4e-4b58-8289-\
eb2b2ae6e054"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"$RecomputePlasmaCorrections", "=", "False"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.861101424879527*^9, 3.861101425464632*^9}, {
  3.861101460931744*^9, 3.8611014615066347`*^9}, {3.861259616291389*^9, 
  3.8612596167137012`*^9}, {3.861261714483686*^9, 3.86126171508357*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[95]:=",ExpressionUUID->"61fe627e-5390-476f-8532-4ee97b7172bf"],

Cell["\<\
Since the QED effects depend on temperature, they need to be computed only \
once for all and they are stored on a file. But we can force the \
recomputation of these corrections by setting $RecomputePlasmaCorrections to \
True;\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.715961498733636*^9, 3.7159615450682487`*^9}, 
   3.7160054044238987`*^9, {3.716005435709878*^9, 
   3.716005439502181*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4b4bfe17-d50e-4974-9cb9-\
67b1e2d6bce4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Neutrinos options", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9361642067260313`*^9, 3.936164212127535*^9}, {
  3.936338938011116*^9, 
  3.936338939743161*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fc8796a1-9c2f-4cdf-80ff-\
be70a254bdff"],

Cell["Neutrinos generations. Usually this is 3.", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7198123234977407`*^9, 3.719812330443225*^9}, {
  3.722683898069311*^9, 3.722683909229216*^9}, {3.9351127106506042`*^9, 
  3.935112712449518*^9}, {3.935113264463097*^9, 
